I regularly get comments or reactions about the kinds of foods my kids eat, and that’s quickly followed by questions about how I did that.
Start with things that are difficult to chew off pieces, gradually work your way towards more chewable foods.
From the beginning, include a variety of foods at the same time, giving babies choices about what they want to eat.
The kids always sat at the dinner table with us, even at the beginning when they were just gnawing on cucumber spears or a piece of toast. We wanted them to see us eating, to be interested in what we were eating.
As we moved through foods, we strived to introduce a variety of foods. We gave them two to three things at a time, and aimed for them all to be different colors (who wants an entire tray of brown food, right?
But let’s be honest – feeding them before they can talk (or more importantly, talk back) is pretty easy. If they don’t like something, they might make a face or throw it off their tray, but how do these ideas hold up during the toddler and preschool years?
While hiking, biking, camping, and boating have been at the top of the list for our family, these activities don’t always sit well with our four and six year old. So how do we get the little ones on board for an outdoor adventure?
Something magical happens when we combine a picnic with an outdoor activity. The kids are more willing to go on an adventure (our code word for any type of strenuous outdoor activity) and complain a lot less on the car ride there.
